Learning GitHub Actions – Day 4: Steps
Continuing my GitHub Actions learning journey as part of my Cloud & DevOps roadmap!

📌 What are Steps in GitHub Actions?
Steps are individual tasks that run inside a job. They help execute commands, perform actions, and automate different stages of a CI/CD pipeline.
Steps within a job execute sequentially on the same runner and can share data with each other.

🛠️ Practical Example:

name: Steps Demo  

on:   
    push:     
          branches:       
              - main  

 jobs:   
     demo:     
          runs-on: ubuntu-latest     
           steps:       
                 - name: Checkout Code         
                    uses: actions/checkout@v4        

                 - name: Display Message        
                    run: echo "Welcome to GitHub Actions!"        
 
                 - name: Check Current Directory         
                    run: pwd        
 
                - name: List Files         
                   run: ls -la

🔍 How it works:
steps: Defines individual tasks inside a job.
name: Gives each step a readable name.
uses: Executes a reusable action.
run: Executes shell commands.
Steps run one after another by default.

💡 Key Takeaway:
Steps break a job into smaller, manageable tasks, making CI/CD workflows easier to understand, execute, and troubleshoot.
